WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Immunity questions often ask you to do one

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:03.800
of three things.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.320
Put the response in order, from the moment

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.960
a pathogen gets in to the moment antibodies

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:09.680
appear.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.120
Read a graph of antibody levels.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.760
Or name the immunity someone has, active or

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.000
passive, natural or artificial.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.680
Each one hides a trap that costs marks.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.240
Here are three HSC-style questions, one of each.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.760
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:28.640
through it together.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:31.466
Question one.

00:00:31.547 --> 00:00:35.226
Pruning secateurs nick a gardener's thumb, and soil

00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:37.146
bacteria get into the cut.

00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:39.546
Eight events are listed out of order.

00:00:39.787 --> 00:00:42.346
Put them in order, from entry to antibody

00:00:42.427 --> 00:00:43.186
production.

00:00:43.387 --> 00:00:47.146
Then explain why antibodies against these bacteria aren't

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.066
found in the blood until about a week

00:00:49.147 --> 00:00:50.146
after the cut.

00:00:50.427 --> 00:00:51.706
Pause here to answer.

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:04.933
Start where the pathogen starts.

00:01:05.494 --> 00:01:08.373
C, the bacteria get past the skin, the

00:01:08.454 --> 00:01:09.413
first barrier.

00:01:09.654 --> 00:01:12.133
Then come the innate defences, which are always

00:01:12.214 --> 00:01:12.533
ready.

00:01:13.094 --> 00:01:16.773
E, damaged cells and mast cells release chemicals

00:01:16.854 --> 00:01:19.813
such as histamine, so small vessels widen and

00:01:19.894 --> 00:01:20.293
leak.

00:01:20.694 --> 00:01:23.973
That's a chemical change causing physical ones, redness

00:01:24.054 --> 00:01:24.773
and swelling.

00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:29.013
G, neutrophils leave the blood and engulf bacteria.

00:01:29.414 --> 00:01:32.293
And F, a dendritic cell carries pieces of

00:01:32.334 --> 00:01:33.893
the bacteria to a lymph node.

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:37.493
That's block, alarm, eat, alert.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.380
Now the adaptive response, in the lymph node.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.060
A, the few B cells whose receptors already

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.860
fit an antigen on the bacteria bind it.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.140
D, a helper T cell, already switched on

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.620
by the dendritic cell, gives them the go-ahead.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.900
H, they divide into a clone, and most

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.100
become plasma cells, while some become memory cells.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.940
B, plasma cells release antibodies, which bind the

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:05.660
bacteria.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.820
So the order is C, E, G, F,

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.140
A, D, H, B.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.940
Notice that the dendritic cell is the bridge

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.460
between the two halves.

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:20.786
Part b asks why it takes about a

00:02:20.867 --> 00:02:21.266
week.

00:02:21.747 --> 00:02:23.986
Only a few B cells fit this antigen

00:02:24.027 --> 00:02:25.026
to begin with.

00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:27.506
They have to meet it, get the go-ahead,

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:29.666
and then divide many times before there are

00:02:29.747 --> 00:02:32.066
enough plasma cells to make antibodies you can

00:02:32.147 --> 00:02:32.546
measure.

00:02:33.187 --> 00:02:36.546
Meanwhile, the innate defences hold the bacteria back.

00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:40.966
The classic mistake is writing that the body

00:02:41.087 --> 00:02:43.606
makes a new antibody to fit the bacteria.

00:02:44.087 --> 00:02:45.766
It doesn't design anything.

00:02:46.247 --> 00:02:48.886
It selects B cells that already fit, and

00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:49.686
copies them.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:52.580
Question two.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.620
A volunteer in a vaccine trial is given

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.900
two doses, on day zero and day twenty-eight.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.700
The graph shows the antibody level in their

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.940
blood, with a dashed line at the level

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.700
that protects against the disease.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.060
Compare the responses to the two doses, using

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.260
data from the graph.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.540
Then explain the difference.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.180
Pause here to answer.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.000
First, check the scale.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.960
Each gridline is ten times the one below

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:30.520
it.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.880
Then find the lag, the flat start before

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.280
the curve rises.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.040
After dose one, the level stays flat for

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.320
about six days, then antibodies peak at about

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.600
forty units on day fourteen, below the protective

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:45.080
line.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.120
After dose two, they rise within about two

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.760
days and peak at about two thousand units

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.120
on day thirty-five.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.320
That's about fifty times higher, and it stays

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.680
above the protective line.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:03:59.940
Now explain it.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.540
Dose one gave a primary response.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.100
Only a few B cells fit the vaccine

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.660
antigen, so they had to be selected and

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.180
multiply, which took days, and some became memory

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:11.740
cells.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:14.940
Dose two is the same antigen, so it

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.540
met a secondary response.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.340
Many memory cells were already waiting.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.940
They became plasma cells quickly, with fewer divisions,

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.820
so antibody rose sooner and much higher.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:28.940
After dose one, the level never reached the

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.980
protective line, so one dose alone wouldn't have

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.340
protected this volunteer.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.060
That's why many vaccines are given as more

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:37.180
than one dose.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.080
The classic mistake is counting gridlines.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.960
On a log scale, two thousand sits less

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.160
than two gridlines above forty, so students write

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.160
that the second peak is about twice as

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:50.640
high.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.240
Read the values off the axis, then divide.

00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:57.426
Question three.

00:04:57.707 --> 00:05:00.266
A wildlife carer is scratched by a flying

00:05:00.347 --> 00:05:01.146
fox.

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:04.826
Some Australian flying foxes carry a lyssavirus, a

00:05:04.907 --> 00:05:06.586
close relative of rabies.

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:10.266
Rabies vaccine and rabies antibodies are used to

00:05:10.347 --> 00:05:13.226
prevent this infection, and they're highly effective in

00:05:13.427 --> 00:05:14.666
almost all cases.

00:05:15.307 --> 00:05:18.866
Once symptoms start, it's almost always fatal.

00:05:19.307 --> 00:05:21.466
The wound is washed with soap and water.

00:05:21.707 --> 00:05:24.586
She's then given an injection of rabies antibodies,

00:05:24.627 --> 00:05:26.826
and the first dose of rabies vaccine.

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:30.666
Identify the type of immunity each injection gives.

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:33.466
Then explain why both are given.

00:05:34.107 --> 00:05:35.386
Pause here to answer.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.080
Use the two questions from the last video.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:50.760
Who made the antibodies?

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.040
The injected antibodies were made by other people's

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.240
immune systems, so they give artificial passive immunity.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:01.080
The vaccine gives her the antigen, so her

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.200
own B cells make antibodies and memory cells.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:07.120
That's artificial active immunity.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.800
Both are artificial, because they come from medical

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:10.680
treatment.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.720
Natural would mean she'd caught the infection, or

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.640
received antibodies from her mother.

00:06:17.667 --> 00:06:19.226
Now why both.

00:06:19.507 --> 00:06:21.506
Her own response to the vaccine takes a

00:06:21.667 --> 00:06:24.226
week or more to make useful antibodies, and

00:06:24.307 --> 00:06:26.786
the virus could already be entering nerves near

00:06:26.867 --> 00:06:27.426
the wound.

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:31.426
The injected antibodies work straight away, binding virus

00:06:31.507 --> 00:06:33.986
at the wound, but they fade over weeks

00:06:34.067 --> 00:06:35.186
and leave no memory.

00:06:35.747 --> 00:06:38.306
So the injected antibodies cover the gap, and

00:06:38.387 --> 00:06:40.626
the vaccine builds lasting protection.

00:06:41.187 --> 00:06:43.986
This works because symptoms usually take weeks or

00:06:44.147 --> 00:06:45.266
months to appear.

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:49.826
The classic mistake is saying the vaccine works

00:06:49.987 --> 00:06:52.946
straight away, so the antibody injection isn't needed.

00:06:53.587 --> 00:06:56.706
A vaccine starts a primary response, and that

00:06:56.787 --> 00:06:57.746
takes time.

00:06:59.767 --> 00:07:01.686
So here's how to start each type.

00:07:02.167 --> 00:07:04.286
To put a response in order, start at

00:07:04.327 --> 00:07:08.246
the barrier, run the innate steps, block, alarm,

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:12.966
eat, alert, then the adaptive ones, fit, go-ahead,

00:07:13.047 --> 00:07:15.526
clone, plasma cells, antibodies.

00:07:16.007 --> 00:07:18.966
For a graph, read the scale, then ask,

00:07:19.047 --> 00:07:20.806
same antigen, or a new one?

00:07:21.287 --> 00:07:24.486
For immunity, ask who made the antibodies, and

00:07:24.567 --> 00:07:26.166
was it natural or medical?

00:07:26.727 --> 00:07:30.326
Next, how hygiene and quarantine interrupt spread.

00:07:32.334 --> 00:07:33.773
Here are the key notes.

00:07:34.014 --> 00:07:35.933
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:36.014 --> 00:07:36.813
to copy them.
